b)\\) lies on the line \\(V_a\\).  \nConsequently the family \\(\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_n\\}\\) satisfies the covering condition with **\\(k=0\\)** sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Replacing vertical lines by sunny ones  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe shall replace the rightmost \\(k\\) vertical lines \\(V_{n-k+1},\\dots ,V_n\\) by \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \nAll other vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\) are kept unchanged; they remain non‑sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  Definition of the sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(j=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_j:\\; y=j\\bigl(x-(n-k)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(S_j\\) is the integer \\(j\\); therefore \\(S_j\\) is **not** parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis (\\(j\\neq0\\)), not parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis (\\(j\\) is finite), and not parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\) (\\(j\\neq-1\\)).  \nHence each \\(S_j\\) is a sunny line, and the \\(k\\) lines are pairwise distinct because they have distinct slopes.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  Points covered by the sunny lines  \n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(a\\ge n-k+1\\) (i.e. the point lies in one of the columns whose vertical line is being removed).  \nWrite  \n\n\\[\nd=a-(n-k)\\qquad (1\\le d\\le k).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have \\(b\\le n+1-a=n+1-(n-k+d)=k+1-d\\).  \nConsequently  \n\n\\[\nb-1\\le k-d .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nChoose \\(j=b\\) (note that \\(1\\le b\\le k\\) by (5)).  \nFor this \\(j\\) we have  \n\n\\[\nj\\bigl(a-(n-k)\\bigr)+1=j d+1=j d+1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(j=b\\) and \\(d=a-(n-k)\\),\n\n\\[\nj d+1 = b d+1 = b\\bigl(a-(n-k)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut from the definition (3) of \\(S_j\\),\n\n\\[\ny=j\\bigl(x-(n-k)\\bigr)+1 .\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=a\\) gives exactly the right–hand side of (6); therefore the point \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_j\\).  \nThus **every** point of \\(T_n\\) whose column index is \\(\\ge n-k+1\\) lies on the sunny line \\(S_{\\,b}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Points covered by the remaining vertical lines  \n\nIf \\(a\\le n-k\\) then \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the column of the unchanged vertical line \\(V_a\\); hence it is covered by that line.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Verification of the construction  \n\n*All points are covered.*  \nFrom §§2.2 and 2.3 each point of \\(T_n\\) lies either on one of the retained vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\) or on one of the sunny lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\).  \n\n*Exactly \\(k\\) lines are sunny.*  \nThe \\(k\\) lines \\(S_1,\\dots ,S_k\\) have slopes \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\); none of these slopes equals \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\).  \nAll other lines in the family are the vertical lines \\(V_1,\\dots ,V_{n-k}\\), whose slopes are \\(\\infty\\).  \nHence precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(S_j\\) are sunny.\n\n*All lines are distinct.*  \nThe vertical lines have distinct \\(x\\)-intercepts, while the sunny lines have distinct slopes; therefore no two of the \\(n\\) lines coincide.\n\nThus for the chosen integer \\(k\\) we have produced \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustion of the possible values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nSince a family of \\(n\\) lines cannot contain more than \\(n\\) sunny lines, the set  \n\n\\[\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\n\\]\n\nis the complete set of admissible values.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**\n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of non‑negative integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]"
