            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{P}_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that must lie on at least one of the chosen\nlines.  \nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), from\n\\(\\infty\\) (vertical), and from \\(-1\\); i.e. it is not parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n .\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) we have \\(1\\le b\\le n\\), so the point lies\non the line \\(y=b\\).  None of these lines is sunny, therefore \\(k=0\\) is\nfeasible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nFor each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = x + i .\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_i\\) has slope \\(1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nFor each integer \\(j\\) with \\(k+1\\le j\\le n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\; y = j .\n\\]\n\nThese are horizontal, hence non‑sunny.\n\nConsider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}_k=\\{L_0,\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\cup\\{H_{k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\},\n\\]\n\nwhich consists of exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines,\nall distinct.\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\).  \nIf \\(b-a\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k-1\\}\\) then \\(b-a=i\\) for some \\(i\\) in that set,\nso \\((a,b)\\) satisfies \\(b=a+i\\) and lies on \\(L_i\\).\n\nOtherwise \\(b-a\\ge k\\).  Because \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\nb\\le n\\quad\\text{and}\\quad b\\ge k+1 .\n\\]\n\nHence \\(b\\) equals one of the integers \\(k+1,\\dots ,n\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies\non the horizontal line \\(H_b\\).\n\nThus every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) belongs to some line of \\(\\mathcal{L}_k\\),\nand exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.  Therefore a configuration with\nany prescribed \\(k\\) in the range \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nNow we need a covering by \\(n\\) sunny lines.  \nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n&L_i:\\; y = x + i \\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\[2mm]\n&S:\\; y = -2x + (n+2).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nAll of them have slopes different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\); hence\neach is sunny, and they are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Points with \\(b-a\\le n-2\\).*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal{P}_n\\) satisfies \\(b-a=i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le n-2\\),\nthen \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(L_i\\).\n\n*The remaining point.*  \nThe equation \\(b-a=n-1\\) together with \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n2a + n -1 \\le n+1 \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; a\\le 1 .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(a\\ge1\\), we have \\(a=1\\) and consequently \\(b=n\\).  Hence the only\npoint of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) with \\(b-a=n-1\\) is \\((1,n)\\).  Substituting\n\\((1,n)\\) into the equation of \\(S\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nn = -2\\cdot 1 + (n+2),\n\\]\n\nso \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(S\\).\n\nTherefore every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\) is covered by one of the \\(n\\)\nsunny lines listed above, and all \\(n\\) lines are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for each integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\), a set of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines that\n\n* cover every point of \\(\\mathcal{P}_n\\), and  \n* contain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nSince \\(k\\) cannot be negative and cannot exceed the total number of lines\n\\(n\\), the set of all possible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**End of solution.**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
