ine the following \\(n\\) sunny lines:\n\n   * **\\(t+1\\) lines of slope \\(1\\):**  \n\n     \\[\n     L_i:\\;y=x+(2i-t)\\qquad(i=0,1,\\dots ,t).\n     \\]\n\n   * **\\(t\\) lines of slope \\(2\\):**  \n\n     \\[\n     M_j:\\;y=2x+(2j-(t+1))\\qquad(j=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n     \\]\n\n   Every line is distinct and none of them is parallel to the\n   \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); hence all are sunny.\n   One checks directly that\n\n   * each \\(L_i\\) contains exactly \\(t+1=\\frac{n+1}{2}\\) points of the triangle,\n   * each \\(M_j\\) also contains exactly \\(t+1\\) points,\n   * the families \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) and \\(\\{M_j\\}\\) are disjoint,\n   * together they contain  \n\n     \\[\n     (t+1)^2+t(t+1)= (2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2\n     \\]\n\n     points, i.e. all points of the required triangle.\n\n   Hence for odd \\(n\\) we have a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   The bound (1) is sharp: the constructions above realise every integer\n   \\(k\\) allowed by (1).  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly the one stated in the answer.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nT_n=|\\mathcal T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to\n\\((1,0),(0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\nu(L)\\) the number of points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).\n\n*If \\(L\\) is non‑sunny* (horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\)),\nthe intersection of \\(L\\) with \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) is a segment of length at most \\(n\\); hence  \n\n\\[\n\\nu(L)\\le n .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n*If \\(L\\) is sunny*, write its primitive integer direction vector as \\((p,q)\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\((p,q)\\notin\\{(1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\}\\).\nMoving from one lattice point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) to the next along \\(L\\) changes the\ncoordinates by \\((p,q)\\); therefore the number of lattice points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) on \\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor\\le\n\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nLet a configuration contain \\(k\\) sunny lines and \\(n-k\\) non‑sunny lines.\nUsing (2) and (3),\n\n\\[\nT_n\\le (n-k)n+k\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\nSolving this inequality for \\(k\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\le\n\\begin{cases}\nn-1, & n\\text{ even},\\\\[2mm]\nn,   & n\\text{ odd}.\n\\end{cases}\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus (4) is a necessary condition for the existence of a required\nconfiguration.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Realising every \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\nTake the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\ny=1,\\;y=2,\\dots ,y=n-k .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny and together contain all points of\n\\(\\mathcal T_n\\) whose second coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).\nThe points that are still uncovered are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal R_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid 1\\le a\\le k,\\;1\\le b\\le k,\\;a+b\\le k+1\\},\n\\]\n\na right‑angled triangular set of side‑length \\(k\\); it has\n\\(|\\mathcal R_k|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2\\) points.\n\nFor each \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal R_k\\) choose a line of slope \\(2\\) passing\nthrough that point; different points give different lines because two\ndifferent points determine a unique line, and a line of slope \\(2\\) is\nnever parallel to any of the three forbidden directions.  \nConsequently we obtain exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines, each covering one point\nof \\(\\mathcal R_k\\).  Together with the lines (5) we have a family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the required covering property and\ncontaining precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nHence for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) a suitable\nconfiguration exists, irrespective of the parity of \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=n\\) (only possible when \\(n\\) is odd)\n\nAssume \\(n=2t+1\\) (\\(t\\ge1\\)).  \nDefine the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y = x + (2i-t) \\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,t),\\\\[2mm]\nM_j &: y = 2x + (2j-(t+1)) \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes are \\(1\\) or \\(2\\); therefore none of the lines in (6) is\nparallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\); each\nline is sunny, and the lines are pairwise distinct.\n\n*Points on the \\(L_i\\).*  \nFor a fixed \\(i\\) the condition \\(y=x+(2i-t)\\) together with\n\\(a+b\\le n+1=2t+2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le t+1,\\qquad b=a+(2i-t),\n\\]\n\nso exactly the \\(t+1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,\\,a+2i-t)\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nbelong to \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  Hence \\(\\nu(L_i)=t+1\\).\n\n*Points on the \\(M_j\\).*  \nFor a fixed \\(j\\) the equation \\(y=2x+(2j-(t+1))\\) together with\n\\(a+b\\le2t+2\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n1\\le a\\le t+1,\\qquad b=2a+2j-(t+1),\n\\]\n\nand again exactly the \\(t+1\\) points  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl\\{(a,\\,2a+2j-(t+1))\\mid a=1,2,\\dots ,t+1\\bigr\\}\n\\]\n\nlie in \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).  Thus \\(\\nu(M_j)=t+1\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint, because a point satisfying both\n\\(y=x+(2i-t)\\) and \\(y=2x+(2j-(t+1))\\) would give\n\\(x=-(t+1)+2i-2j\\), which is impossible for positive integer\ncoordinates inside \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\nConsequently the total number of covered points is  \n\n\\[\n(t+1)^2+t(t+1)=(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2=T_n,\n\\]\n\ni.e. every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) lies on one of the lines (6).\nThus for odd \\(n\\) we have a configuration consisting solely of sunny\nlines, i.e. with \\(k=n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nThe inequality (4) shows that no configuration can have more than the\nstated number of sunny lines.  The constructions of Sections 2 and 3\nshow that every integer allowed by (4) actually occurs.\n\nTherefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{array}{c}\nk=0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is even},\\\\[2mm]\nk=0,1,\\dots ,n\\quad\\text{if }n\\text{ is odd}.\n\\end{array}}\n\\]"
