tion with \\(k=n\\) sunny\n   lines.  This is possible only when \\(n\\) is odd, in agreement with the\n   bound (1).\n\n4. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (1) gives a necessary condition for the existence of a\n   configuration; the constructions of Sections\u202f2 and\u202f3 show that the\n   condition is also sufficient.  Therefore the admissible values of\n   \\(k\\) are exactly those stated in the answer box.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n### Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^{2}\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad\nT_n=|\\mathcal T_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its direction vector is not parallel to\n\\((1,0),(0,1)\\) or \\((1,-1)\\); equivalently its slope is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFor a line \\(L\\) denote by \\(\\nu(L)\\) the number of points of \\(\\mathcal\nT_n\\) that lie on \\(L\\).\n\n*Non\u2011sunny lines.*  \nIf \\(L\\) is horizontal, vertical or of slope \\(-1\\), its intersection with\nthe right\u2011angled triangle \\(\\{(x,y)\\mid x>0,\\;y>0,\\;x+y\\le n+1\\}\\) is a\nsegment of length at most \\(n\\); consequently  \n\n\\[\n\\nu(L)\\le n .\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nWrite a primitive integer direction vector of \\(L\\) as \\((p,q)\\) with\n\\(\\gcd(p,q)=1\\) and \\((p,q)\\notin\\{(1,0),(0,1),(1,-1)\\}\\).\nMoving from one lattice point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) to the next along \\(L\\)\nchanges the coordinates by \\((p,q)\\); therefore the number of lattice\npoints of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) on \\(L\\) is at most  \n\n\\[\n1+\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n}{\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}}\\Bigr\\rfloor\n\\le\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\n(The inequality is strict unless \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}=1\\); the only primitive\nvectors with \\(\\max\\{|p|,|q|\\}=1\\) that are not forbidden are \\((1,