y\n\\(k\\) sunny lines.  Hence for every \\(k\\le n-1\\) such a configuration\nexists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=n\\) (only possible when \\(n\\) is odd)\n\nAssume \\(n\\) is odd and write \\(n=2t+1\\) with \\(t\\ge1\\).\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_i &: y = x + (2i-t) \\qquad (i=0,1,\\dots ,t),\\\\[2mm]\nM_j &: y = 2x + (2j-(t+1)) \\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,t).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{12}\n\\]\n\nTheir slopes are \\(1\\) and \\(2\\); thus none is parallel to the\n\\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\).  The lines are clearly\ndifferent.\n\n*Points on the \\(L_i\\).*  \nSolving \\(y=x+(2i-t)\\) together with the condition \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\) gives  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t+1\\},\\qquad b=a+(2i-t),\n\\]\n\nso each \\(L_i\\) contains exactly \\(t+1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\n*Points on the \\(M_j\\).*  \nFrom \\(y=2x+(2j-(t+1))\\) and \\(a+b\\le2t+2\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\na\\in\\{1,\\dots ,t+1\\},\\qquad b=2a+2j-(t+1),\n\\]\n\nagain \\(t+1\\) points of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) on each \\(M_j\\).\n\nThe two families are disjoint: a point satisfying both equations would\nimply \\(x=-(t+1)+2i-2j\\le0\\), contradicting the positivity of the\ncoordinates in \\(\\mathcal T_n\\).\n\nHence the total number of covered points equals  \n\n\\[\n(t+1)^2+t(t+1)=(2t+1)(t+1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2,\n\\]\n\ni.e. every point of \\(\\mathcal T_n\\) lies on one of the lines (12).  All\n\\(n\\) lines are sunny, giving a configuration with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.\nThis construction works only when \\(n\\) is odd, exactly matching the\nnecessary bound (8).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Completion of the proof  \n\nInequality (8) shows that no configuration can have more sunny lines\nthan stated.  The constructions of Sections\u202f2 and\u202f3 realise every\ninteger allowed by (8).  Consequently the set of admissible valu