            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nbe the set of lattice points that have to lie on at least one of the chosen lines.\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are always possible  \n\n* \\(k=0\\).  \n  The \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n\n  \\tag{2}\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, none of them is sunny, and for any \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) we have\n  \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \n\n* \\(k=1\\).  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (all non‑sunny).  \n  The only point of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered is \\((n,1)\\).  \n  The line through \\((n,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) has equation  \n\n  \\[\n  y-1=\\frac{1}{1-n}(x-n)\\qquad\\Longleftrightarrow\\qquad y=\\frac{x}{1-n}+ \\frac{n}{1-n},\n  \\]\n\n  whose slope \\(\\dfrac{1}{1-n}\\) is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) for every\n  \\(n\\ge 3\\); therefore it is sunny.  The resulting family consists of\n  exactly one sunny line.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 3\\) the values \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\) are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(n=3\\)\n\nHere  \n\n\\[\nT_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe examine all possibilities for the number of sunny lines.\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  The three vertical lines \\(x=1,2,3\\) cover \\(T_3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Take the two vertical lines \\(x=1,2\\) and the sunny line through\n  \\((3,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)).  All six points are covered.\n\n* **\\(k=3\\).**  \n  The three sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  \\begin{aligned}\n  L_1 &: y=x      &&\\text{(covers }(1,1),(2,2)),\\\\\n  L_2 &: y=-\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac52 &&\\text{(covers }(1,2),(3,1)),\\\\\n  L_3 &: y=-2x+5 &&\\text{(covers }(2,1),(1,3))\n  \\end{aligned}\n  \\]\n\n  are pairwise distinct, none is parallel to the forbidden directions,\n  and together they contain every point of \\(T_3\\).\n\n* **\\(k=2\\) is impossible.**  \n  Suppose a covering with exactly two sunny lines exists.\n  Any non‑sunny line must be vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).\n  If a vertical line is used, it can contain at most the three points\n  with the same first coordinate.  Removing such a line leaves three points\n  whose pairwise slopes are \\(0,\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently none of them\n  can be joined by a sunny line, forcing at least three sunny lines – a\n  contradiction.  The same contradiction appears if the non‑sunny line is\n  horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\).  Hence no covering with exactly two sunny\n  lines exists.\n\nConsequently for \\(n=3\\) the admissible values are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\in\\{0,1,3\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The case \\(n\\ge 4\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  \nWe construct \\(n\\) distinct lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny part  \n\nIf \\(k<n\\) take the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny and cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate does not exceed \\(n-k\\).  When \\(k=n\\) the set (4) is empty.\n\n#### 3.2  Reduction to a smaller triangle  \n\nAfter deleting the points already covered, the remaining set is  \n\n\\[\nT'_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge n-k+1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nIntroduce new variables  \n\n\\[\nu=a-(n-k),\\qquad v=b .\n\\]\n\nThen \\(u\\ge 1,\\;v\\ge 1\\) and \\(u+v\\le k+1\\); hence  \n\n\\[\nT'_k=\\{(u,v)\\mid u\\ge 1,\\;v\\ge 1,\\;u+v\\le k+1\\},\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nwhich is a triangular lattice of side length \\(k\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Covering the small triangle with \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; v = -\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,u + \\frac{k+1}{k+1-i}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) equals \\(-\\dfrac{i}{k+1-i}\\); because \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) we have\n\\(-\\dfrac{i}{k+1-i}\\neq 0,\\infty,-1\\).  Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny, and the\n\\(k\\) lines are distinct (different slopes).\n\nA point \\((u,v)\\) satisfying \\(u+v\\le k+1\\) belongs to exactly one of the\n\\(L_i\\): solving (7) for \\(i\\) gives \\(i=u\\).  Therefore the family\n\\(\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\}\\) covers the whole set (6).\n\n#### 3.4  Lifting back to the original coordinates  \n\nReplacing \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) by \\(a-(n-k)\\) and \\(b\\) respectively, each line\n\\(L_i\\) becomes a line in the original plane; together with the vertical\nlines (4) we obtain a collection of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which\nprecisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one\nof them.\n\nThus for every \\(n\\ge 4\\) and every \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) a suitable\nfamily of lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Final answer\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\nThe construction