mall triangle with \\(k\\) sunny lines \u2013 **Critical Error**\n* The solution defines, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\),\n  \\[\n  L_i:\\; v = -\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,u + \\frac{k+1}{k+1-i}.\n  \\tag{7}\n  \\]\n* **Error 1 \u2013 Forbidden slopes:**  \n  For \\(i=k-1\\) we have  \n  \\[\n  -\\frac{i}{k+1-i}= -\\frac{k-1}{2},\n  \\]\n  which equals \\(-1\\) when \\(k=3\\). More generally, when \\(i=k-1\\) and \\(k=2\\) the slope is \\(-1\\); for any \\(k\\ge2\\) the slope can be \\(-1\\) (e.g., \\(k=3,i=2\\)). Since a sunny line must avoid slope \\(-1\\), the claim \u201cthe slope \u2026 is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)\u201d is false. Consequently **not all \\(L_i\\) are sunny**.\n\n* **Error 2 \u2013 Incorrect covering claim:**  \n  The solution asserts that a point \\((u,v)\\) with \\(u+v\\le k+1\\) belongs to exactly one of the lines \\(L_i\\), and that solving (7) for \\(i\\) yields \\(i=u\\).  \n  Solving (7) for \\(i\\) gives  \n  \\[\n  i = \\frac{(k+1)(v-1)}{v-u},\n  \\]\n  which depends on both \\(u\\) and \\(v\\) and is not equal to \\(u\\) in general. For instance, with \\(k=3\\) the point \\((u,v)=(2,1)\\) satisfies \\(u+v=3\\le4\\) but substituting into (7) shows that it does **not** lie on any of the three lines defined above. Hence the claimed \u201cexactly one\u201d property is false; many points of the reduced triangle are uncovered.\n\n* Because the covering of the reduced triangle fails, the subsequent step of \u201clifting back\u201d cannot produce a valid family of lines for the original problem.\n\nThus the construction for \\(n\\ge4\\) is invalid, and the conclusion that **all** integers \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) are attainable is unsupported.\n\n#### 3.4  Lifting back \u2013 dependent on the flawed construction\n* Since the set of lines \\(\\{L_i\\}\\) does not correctly cover the reduced triangle, the lifted lines do not guarantee coverage of the original set \\(T_n\\). Consequently the claim that a suitable family exists for 