------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\) (\\(0\\le k\\le n\\))\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n1. **Non‑sunny part.**  \n   Take the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   V_1,V_2,\\dots ,V_m,\\qquad V_i:\\;x=i .\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1 each of them is non‑sunny, they are distinct, and together they\n   contain every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le m\\).\n\n2. **Sunny part.**  \n   For each integer \\(a\\) with \\(m+1\\le a\\le n\\) choose a real number\n   \\(r_a\\) such that \\(r_a\\notin\\{0,-1\\}\\) and the numbers \\(\\{r_a\\}\\) are pairwise\n   distinct (e.g. \\(r_a=a\\)).  \n   Define  \n\n   \\[\n   L_a:\\;y=r_a(x-a)+1 .\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 2 each \\(L_a\\) is sunny.  The point \\((a,1)\\) belongs to \\(T_n\\) and\n   satisfies the equation of \\(L_a\\); consequently \\(L_a\\) covers at least one\n   point of the column \\(x=a\\).  Because the slopes \\(r_a\\) are distinct, the\n   lines \\(\\{L_a\\mid a=m+1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) are pairwise distinct and none of them\n   coincides with any vertical line.\n\n3. **The whole family.**  \n   Set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\mathcal L:=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_m\\}\\cup\\{L_{m+1},\\dots ,L_n\\}.\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n\n   The family \\(\\mathcal L\\) contains exactly \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, of which\n   precisely the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{m+1},\\dots ,L_n\\) are sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 3 (Coverage of all points of \\(T_n\\) by \\(\\mathcal L\\))\n\nEvery point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\(1\\le a\\le m\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(V_a\\).  \nIf \\(a\\ge m+1\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the sunny line \\(L_a\\) because\n\\(L_a\\) contains the whole vertical line \\(x=a\\) (all points with that\n\\(x\\)-coordinate satisfy the equation \\(y=r_a(x-a)+1\\) when \\(x=a\\)).  Hence in\nboth cases the point is covered. ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Completion of the proof\n\nFor any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) the construction above yields a set\n\\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the covering condition\n(Lemma 3) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  Therefore every such\n\\(k\\) is admissible.\n\nConversely, the definition of “sunny” imposes no restriction that would forbid\nany particular number of sunny lines; the explicit constructions show that\nall numbers from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\) can indeed be realized.\n\nHence the complete set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
