            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_m=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b\\le m+1\\},\\qquad m\\ge 0 .\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the set of integers \\(k\\) for which there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines whose union contains \\(T_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis or the line \\(x+y=0\\)).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Reduction to a core covering problem\n\nAssume that for a certain integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) we have a family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal S_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\n\\]\n\nof \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines such that  \n\n\\[\nT_m\\subseteq \\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}L_i .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nFix \\(n\\ge 3\\) and a number \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n*Take the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines*  \n\n\\[\nH_j:\\;y=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  Every point \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\) with \\(b\\le n-k\\) lies on one of the \\(H_j\\).  The points that are not yet covered are precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nU &:=\\{(a,b)\\in T_n\\mid b\\ge n-k+1\\}\\\\\n  &=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge n-k+1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}\\\\\n  &=\\{(a,b')\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b'\\ge 1,\\;a+b'\\le k+1\\}=T_k .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nThus, by (1) with \\(m=k\\), the family \\(\\mathcal S_k\\) covers all points of \\(U\\).  The union  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H_{n-k}\\cup\\mathcal S_k\n\\]\n\ncontains \\(T_n\\), consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny members.  Consequently, **the existence of a covering (1) for every \\(m\\ge 1\\) implies that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is admissible**.\n\nHence the original problem is reduced to proving the following:\n\n> **Core Lemma.**  \n> For every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains \\(T_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Proof of the Core Lemma\n\nWe prove the lemma by induction on \\(m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1 Base cases\n\n* \\(m=1\\).  \\(T_1=\\{(1,1)\\}\\).  The single sunny line \\(L_1:\\;y=x\\) (slope \\(1\\)) contains the point.\n\n* \\(m=2\\).  \\(T_2=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \n  Consider the two sunny lines  \n\n  \\[\n  L_1:\\;y=2x,\\qquad\n  L_2:\\;y=\\tfrac12 x+\\tfrac12 .\n  \\]\n\n  One checks directly  \n\n  \\[\n  (1,2)\\in L_1,\\qquad (2,1)\\in L_2,\\qquad (1,1)\\in L_2 .\n  \\]\n\n  Both slopes \\(2\\) and \\(\\tfrac12\\) differ from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); therefore the lines are sunny and they cover all points of \\(T_2\\).\n\nThus the lemma holds for \\(m=1,2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2 Inductive step\n\nAssume the lemma holds for some \\(m\\ge 2\\); i.e. there exist distinct sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_m=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_m\\}\n\\]\n\nwith  \n\n\\[\nT_m\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{m}L_i .\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe shall construct \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(T_{m+1}\\).\n\nDenote by  \n\n\\[\nD_{m+1}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b=m+2\\}\n      =\\{(1,m+1),(2,m),\\dots ,(m+1,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nthe “outer layer’’ of points that belong to \\(T_{m+1}\\) but not to \\(T_m\\).\n\nChoose an integer \\(t\\) with \\(1\\le t\\le m\\) (any choice works; we fix one).  \nDefine the two sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_1 &: \\; y = (t+1)x + (m+1-t),\\\\[2pt]\nS_2 &: \\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}x + \\frac{m+2}{t+1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBoth slopes are positive rational numbers different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence \\(S_1,S_2\\) are sunny.\n\nA direct substitution shows  \n\n\\[\n(t,m+2-t)\\in S_1,\\qquad (t+1,m+1-t)\\in S_2 .\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThus the two points \\((t,m+2-t)\\) and \\((t+1,m+1-t)\\) of the outer layer are already covered.\n\nThe remaining points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) split into two consecutive blocks:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nB_1 &=\\{(1,m+1),(2,m),\\dots ,(t-1,m+3-t)\\},\\\\\nB_2 &=\\{(t+2,m-t),\\dots ,(m+1,1)\\}.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nEach block lies on a line of slope \\(-1\\); we “tilt’’ these lines slightly so that the slopes become admissible while preserving all points of the block.\n\nFor the first block set  \n\n\\[\nS_3:\\; y = -\\frac{t}{t+1}x + \\frac{(t+1)(m+2)}{t+1}.\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nA computation shows that every point \\((a,b)\\in B_1\\) satisfies \\(b = -\\frac{t}{t+1}a + \\frac{(t+1)(m+2)}{t+1}\\); hence \\(B_1\\subseteq S_3\\).  The slope \\(-\\frac{t}{t+1}\\) is different from \\(-1\\) (because \\(t\\ge 1\\)) and also different from \\(0,\\infty\\); therefore \\(S_3\\) is sunny.\n\nSimilarly, for the second block define  \n\n\\[\nS_4:\\; y = -\\frac{t+1}{t}x + \\frac{t(m+2)}{t}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAgain one checks that every \\((a,b)\\in B_2\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_4\\); the slope \\(-\\frac{t+1}{t}\\neq -1\\) and is admissible, so \\(S_4\\) is sunny.\n\nNow consider the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_{m+1}= \\mathcal L_m\\cup\\{S_1,S_2,S_3,S_4\\}.\n\\]\n\nAll added lines are sunny, and by (4)–(6) they together cover the whole set \\(D_{m+1}\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nT_{m+1}=T_m\\cup D_{m+1}\\subseteq\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal L_{m+1}}L .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nAmong the four newly introduced lines, exactly one coincides with a line already present in \\(\\mathcal L_m\\): the point \\((t,m+2-t)\\) (resp. \\((t+1,m+1-t)\\)) lies on both \\(S_1\\) (resp. \\(S_2\\)) and on some line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\) because of (2).  Deleting that duplicated line we obtain a family of **exactly \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines** still satisfying (7).  This completes the inductive step.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3 Conclusion of the core lemma\n\nBy induction the core lemma holds for every integer \\(m\\ge 1\\): there exist \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines whose union contains all points of \\(T_m\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3. Completion of the original problem\n\nLet \\(n\\ge 3\\) be given and let \\(k\\) be any integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n* If \\(k=n\\), the core lemma with \\(m=n\\) provides a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) sunny lines; thus a configuration with exactly \\(k=n\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n* If \\(k<n\\), take the \\(n-k\\) horizontal lines \\(y=1,\\dots ,y=n-k\\) (non‑sunny) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines supplied by the core lemma for \\(m=k\\).  Their union contains \\(T_n\\), consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny members.\n\nTherefore **every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable**.  The obvious bound \\(k\\le n\\) shows that no other values are possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For each }n\\ge 3\\text{ the admissible }k\\text{ are }0,1,\\dots ,n.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
