ction for \\(m+1\\)\n\n#### 5.1 Definition of the outer layer\n\\(D_{m+1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}\\mid a+b=m+2\\}\\) is correctly identified as the set of points that need to be added when passing from \\(T_m\\) to \\(T_{m+1}\\).\n\n#### 5.2 Introduction of two lines \\(S_1,S_2\\)\nThe solution defines\n\\[\nS_1:\\; y = (t+1)x + (m+1-t),\\qquad\nS_2:\\; y = \\frac{t}{t+1}x + \\frac{m+2}{t+1},\n\\]\nwhere \\(1\\le t\\le m\\).  \nBoth slopes are positive and different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus the lines are sunny.  \nA direct substitution shows that \\((t,m+2-t)\\in S_1\\) and \\((t+1,m+1-t)\\in S_2\\). This part is **correct**.\n\n#### 5.3 Partition of the remaining points\nThe remaining points of \\(D_{m+1}\\) are split into\n\\[\nB_1 =\\{(1,m+1),(2,m),\\dots ,(t-1,m+3-t)\\},\n\\qquad\nB_2 =\\{(t+2,m-t),\\dots ,(m+1,1)\\}.\n\\]\nThese definitions are accurate.\n\n#### 5.4 Claim about line \\(S_3\\) covering \\(B_1\\)\n\n> \u201cFor the first block set  \n> \\[\n> S_3:\\; y = -\\frac{t}{t+1}x + \\frac{(t+1)(m+2)}{t+1}.\n> \\tag{5}\n> \\]  \n> A computation shows that every point \\((a,b)\\in B_1\\) satisfies \\(b = -\\frac{t}{t+1}a + \\frac{(t+1)(m+2)}{t+1}\\); hence \\(B_1\\subseteq S_3\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nTake a generic point \\((a,b)\\in B_1\\). By definition of \\(B_1\\) we have \\(b=m+2-a\\). Substituting into the right\u2011hand side of the claimed equation gives\n\\[\n-\\frac{t}{t+1}a + (m+2) = m+2 - a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n-\\frac{t}{t+1}a = -a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{t}{t+1}a = a.\n\\]\nSince \\(a\\ge1\\) and \\(t\\ge1\\), \\(\\frac{t}{t+1}\\neq1\\); the equality fails for every \\(a\\ge1\\). Hence **no point of \\(B_1\\) lies on \\(S_3\\)**, contradicting the claim. The algebraic verification is simply wrong, and the construction does not achieve the intended coverage.\n\n#### 5.5 Claim about line \\(S_4\\) covering \\(B_2\\)\n\n> \u201cSimilarly, for the second block define  \n> \\[\n> S_4:\\; y = -\\frac{t+1}{t}x + \\frac{t(m+2)}{t}.\n> \\tag{6}\n> \\]  \n> Again one checks that every \\((a,b)\\in B_2\\) satisfies the equation of \\(S_4\\).\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nA point \\((a,b)\\in B_2\\) satisfies \\(b=m+2-a\\) as well (the same linear relation). Substituting into the equation of \\(S_4\\) yields\n\\[\n-\\frac{t+1}{t}a + (m+2) = m+2 - a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n-\\frac{t+1}{t}a = -a \\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad\n\\frac{t+1}{t}a = a,\n\\]\nwhich again is false for any \\(a\\ge1\\). Thus **\\(S_4\\) does not contain any point of \\(B_2\\)**. The claimed coverage is invalid.\n\nBecause both \\(S_3\\) and \\(S_4\\) fail to cover their respective blocks, the inductive step does **not** produce a set of lines covering \\(D_{m+1}\\). Consequently the induction cannot be completed, and the Core Lemma remains unproved.\n\n#### 5.6 Claim about deleting a duplicated line\n\n> \u201cAmong the four newly introduced lines, exactly one coincides with a line already present in \\(\\mathcal L_m\\): \u2026 Deleting that duplicated line we obtain a family of exactly \\(m+1\\) distinct sunny lines \u2026\u201d\n\n**Critical Error.**  \nThe only guarantee provided by the induction hypothesis (2) is that each point of \\(T_m\\) lies on *some* line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\). From the fact that a new line \\(S_1\\) (or \\(S_2\\)) passes through a point of \\(T_m\\) we can only infer that the point is also on a line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\); we cannot infer that the *entire* line \\(S_1\\) coincides with a line of \\(\\mathcal L_m\\). Therefore we cannot delete a line while preserving coverage, and the argument for obtaining exactly \\(m+1\\) distinct lines is unfounded.\n\n### Step\u202f6 \u2013 Consequence for the Original Problem\nSince the Core Lemma is not established (the inductive construction is incorrect), the subsequent conclusion that **every** \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable lacks a valid foundation. The reduction in Step\u202f1 is sound, but it depends critically on the Core Lemma, which fails.\n\n### Overall Assessment\nThe solution contains multiple **Critical Errors** in the inductive construction of the covering sunny lines. These errors invalidate the core lemma, and consequently the final answer that all integers \\(k\\) from \\(0\\) to \\(n\\) are possible is not justified. The solution is therefore **incorrect**."

