cal P_n\\) belongs to at least one line of \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\):\n\n* Points with second coordinate \\(1\\) lie on \\(H_1\\).\n* For each \\(i\\in\\{2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) the point \\((1,i)\\) lies on \\(S_i\\); the point \\((i+1,1)\\) also lies on \\(S_i\\) (and is already on \\(H_1\\)).\n* The point \\((1,n)\\) lies on \\(S_n\\), and the point \\((2,2)\\) also lies on \\(S_n\\).\n\nAll remaining points have coordinates \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a\\ge2\\) and \\(b\\ge2\\); such a point can be written either as \\((i+1,1)\\) for some \\(i\\) (already covered) or as \\((1,i)\\) for some \\(i\\) (covered by \\(S_i\\)). Hence every element of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) is covered.  \nTherefore a configuration with exactly \\(n-1\\) sunny lines exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Intermediate values \\(0<k<n-1\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0<k<n-1\\).  \nStart from the family \\(\\mathcal L_{n-1}\\) described in Section\u202f3.  \nReplace the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\nH_2:y=2,\\;H_3:y=3,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}:y=n-k\n\\]\n\nby the sunny lines \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{\\,n-k}\\) defined in (6).  \nAfter this replacement we obtain the family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L_k=\n\\bigl\\{H_1,\\;H_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\bigr\\}\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\bigl\\{S_2,\\dots ,S_{\\,n-k},S_{n-1},S_n\\bigr\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe family contains exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines:\n\n* the non\u2011sunny lines are \\(H_1, H_{\\,n-k+1},\\dots ,H_n\\) \u2013 there are \\(n-k\\) of them;\n* the sunny lines are \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{\\,n-k},S_{n-1},S_n\\) \u2013 there are \\(k\\) of them.\n\nThe verification of coverage is identical to that in Section\u202f3, because the only lines that have been altered are the horizontals \\(H_2,\\dots ,H_{\\,n-k}\\), which are now replaced by the sunny lines \\(S_2,\\dots ,S_{\\,n-k}\\) that already contain the points \\((1,i)\\) for the corresponding indices \\(i\\). Consequently every point of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) remains covered.\n\nThus for ev