m (2.1) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk=n-r\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nHence no covering can contain more than\n\\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A construction attaining the upper bound  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\nt:=n-m=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  The non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake the following \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n* the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m-1; \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]\n\n* the diagonal  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=n+1. \\tag{4.2}\n  \\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(\\le m-1\\) lies on one of the\nvertical lines (4.1).  The points with \\(x\\ge m\\) and lying on the diagonal are\ncovered by (4.2).  The only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are\n\n\\[\nU_t:=\\{(a,b)\\mid m\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a,\\;a+b\\neq n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-m+1,b)\\), the set \\(U_t\\) becomes exactly\n\\(T_t\\); therefore \\(|U_t|=\\frac{t(t+1)}2\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1-m\\le c\\le t-m\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x+c . \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines (4.3) have slope \\(1\\); hence they are sunny.  Moreover, for a fixed\n\\(c\\) the points of \\(U_t\\) satisfying \\(y=x+c\\) are precisely those with\n\\(a-b=c\\), i.e. a whole diagonal of the small triangle \\(U_t\\).  Consequently\nthe \\(t\\) lines \\(\\{L_c\\mid 1-m\\le c\\le t-m\\}\\) cover every point of \\(U_t\\).\n\nThus we have constructed a covering of \\(T_n\\) by\n\n* \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines (4.1)–(4.2);\n* \\(t\\) sunny lines (4.3).\n\nHence the number of sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk=t=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\]\n\nshowing that the bound (3.1) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Obtaining all smaller numbers of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the config