"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible numbers of sunny lines are exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\ni.e. all integers from \\(0\\) up to \\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   \\[\n   T_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}\n   \\qquad\\bigl(|T_n|=\\tfrac{n(n+1)}2\\bigr).\n   \\]\n\n2. **A lower bound for the number of non\u2011sunny lines.**  \n   Let a covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines contain \\(r\\) non\u2011sunny lines\n   (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).  \n   By induction on \\(n\\) we prove  \n\n   \\[\n   r\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently the number \\(k=n-r\\) of sunny lines satisfies  \n\n   \\[\n   k\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n     =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Construction attaining the upper bound.**  \n   Put  \n\n   \\[\n   m=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\n   t=n-m=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  \n   Use the \\(m-1\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m-1\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   together with the diagonal line  \n\n   \\[\n   x+y=n+1 .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   These \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) except the set  \n\n   \\[\n   U_t=\\{(a,b)\\mid m\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a,\\;a+b\\neq n+1\\},\n   \\]\n\n   which is exactly a copy of \\(T_t\\) (the same triangular shape, side length\n   \\(t\\)) after the translation \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-m+1,b)\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  \n   The set \\(U_t\\) can be covered by the \\(t\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   y=x+(1-m),\\;y=x+(2-m),\\dots ,y=x+(t-m)\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n\n   (all have slope \\(1\\), hence are sunny).  \n   Thus we have \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny and \\(t\\) sunny lines, i.e. exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   k=t=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n   \\]\n\n4. **All intermediate values of \\(k\\).**  \n   Starting from the configuration that realises the maximal number\n   \\(k_{\\max}= \\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) of sunny lines, replace any sunny line\n   by a vertical line that passes through one of its points (the new line is\n   non\u2011sunny and distinct from the previous ones).  \n   Each replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by one while still\n   covering all points of \\(T_n\\).  Repeating the operation we obtain coverings\n   with any number \\(k\\) of sunny lines satisfying  \n\n   \\[\n   0\\le k\\le k_{\\max}.\n   \\]\n\nHence the set of all possible numbers of sunny lines is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) define  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nGeometrically \\(T_n\\) is the set of lattice points inside the right\u2011angled\nisosceles triangle with vertices \\((1,1),(n,1),(1,n)\\); its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nA line is called **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\); otherwise it is **non\u2011sunny** (vertical, horizontal or of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\nLet a family \\(\\mathcal L=\\{L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\}\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines cover\n\\(T_n\\); denote by  \n\n\\[\nr:=|\\{L_i\\in\\mathcal L\\mid L_i\\text{ is non\u2011sunny}\\}|\n\\qquad\\text{and}\\qquad\nk:=n-r\n\\]\n\nthe numbers of non\u2011sunny and sunny lines, respectively.  \nOur aim is to determine all possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  A lower bound for the number of non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nWe prove by induction on \\(n\\) that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{r\\ge\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil}\\tag{2.1}\n\\]\n\nholds for every covering of \\(T_n\\) by \\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\n#### Base \\(n=3\\)\n\n\\(T_3=\\{(1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\}\\).  \nWith three distinct lines at least two of them must be non\u2011sunny, because a\nsunny line can contain at most one of the three points (the three points are\npairwise non\u2011collinear unless the line is vertical, horizontal or of slope\n\\(-1\\)).  Hence \\(r\\ge2=\\lceil4/2\\rceil\\).\n\n#### Inductive step  \n\nAssume (2.1) true for some \\(n-1\\ge3\\) and consider a covering of \\(T_n\\) by\n\\(n\\) distinct lines.\n\nThe point \\((1,n)\\) lies on the hypotenuse \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \nIf the line of the covering that contains \\((1,n)\\) is **not** the diagonal\n\\(x+y=n+1\\), then that line is sunny and can contain at most one point of the\nhypotenuse (any two distinct points of the hypotenuse determine the diagonal).\nConsequently the whole hypotenuse, which consists of \\(n\\) points, would need\n\\(n\\) distinct lines, contradicting the fact that we have only \\(n\\) lines in\ntotal while at least one of them is already used elsewhere.  Therefore the\ndiagonal  \n\n\\[\nD:\\;x+y=n+1\n\\tag{2.2}\n\\]\n\nmust belong to the family; it is a non\u2011sunny line.\n\nRemove from \\(T_n\\) all points lying on \\(D\\).  The remaining points are exactly\n\n\\[\nT_{n-1}=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n\\},\n\\]\n\nand the remaining lines are \\(n-1\\) distinct lines (the original family without\n\\(D\\)).  Applying the induction hypothesis to this smaller configuration we\nobtain that at least \\(\\lceil\\frac{(n-1)+1}{2}\\rceil=\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\rceil\\)\nof those \\(n-1\\) lines are non\u2011sunny.  Adding the diagonal \\(D\\) gives\n\n\\[\nr\\ge 1+\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil .\n\\]\n\nThus (2.1) holds for \\(n\\), completing the induction.\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Consequence for the number of sunny lines  \n\nFrom (2.1) we obtain\n\n\\[\nk=n-r\\le n-\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil\n   =\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\tag{3.1}\n\\]\n\nHence no covering can contain more than\n\\(\\displaystyle\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\) sunny lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  A construction attaining the upper bound  \n\nSet  \n\n\\[\nm:=\\Bigl\\lceil\\frac{n+1}{2}\\Bigr\\rceil ,\\qquad\nt:=n-m=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor .\n\\]\n\n#### 4.1  The non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the following \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines:\n\n* the vertical lines  \n\n  \\[\n  x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m-1; \\tag{4.1}\n  \\]\n\n* the diagonal  \n\n  \\[\n  x+y=n+1. \\tag{4.2}\n  \\]\n\nEvery point of \\(T_n\\) whose \\(x\\)-coordinate is \\(\\le m-1\\) lies on one of the\nvertical lines (4.1).  The points with \\(x\\ge m\\) and lying on the diagonal are\ncovered by (4.2).  The only points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are\n\n\\[\nU_t:=\\{(a,b)\\mid m\\le a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a,\\;a+b\\neq n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIf we translate \\((a,b)\\mapsto (a-m+1,b)\\), the set \\(U_t\\) becomes exactly\n\\(T_t\\); therefore \\(|U_t|=\\frac{t(t+1)}2\\).\n\n#### 4.2  The sunny lines  \n\nFor each integer \\(c\\) with \\(1-m\\le c\\le t-m\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;y=x+c . \\tag{4.3}\n\\]\n\nAll lines (4.3) have slope \\(1\\); hence they are sunny.  Moreover, for a fixed\n\\(c\\) the points of \\(U_t\\) satisfying \\(y=x+c\\) are precisely those with\n\\(a-b=c\\), i.e. a whole diagonal of the small triangle \\(U_t\\).  Consequently\nthe \\(t\\) lines \\(\\{L_c\\mid 1-m\\le c\\le t-m\\}\\) cover every point of \\(U_t\\).\n\nThus we have constructed a covering of \\(T_n\\) by\n\n* \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny lines (4.1)\u2013(4.2);\n* \\(t\\) sunny lines (4.3).\n\nHence the number of sunny lines equals  \n\n\\[\nk=t=\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\Bigr\\rfloor ,\n\\]\n\nshowing that the bound (3.1) is attainable.\n\n---\n\n### 5.  Obtaining all smaller numbers of sunny lines  \n\nStarting from the configuration of Section\u202f4, which contains the maximal\npossible number \\(k_{\\max}= \\lfloor (n-1)/2\\rfloor\\) of sunny lines, we can\ndecrease the number of sunny lines one by one:\n\n*Pick a sunny line \\(L_c\\) of (4.3).  Choose any point \\(P\\) of \\(L_c\\) that\nlies in \\(U_t\\).  Replace \\(L_c\\) by the vertical line \\(x=\\text{the }x\\text{-coordinate of }P\\).  \nThe new line is non\u2011sunny, distinct from all previously used lines, and still\ncovers the point \\(P\\).  All other points remain covered because the removed\nsunny line covered only points of the same vertical line, which are now covered\nby the new vertical line.*\n\nEach such replacement reduces the number of sunny lines by exactly one while\npreserving a covering of all points of \\(T_n\\).  Repeating the operation we\nobtain coverings with any prescribed number  \n\n\\[\nk\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,k_{\\max}\\}.\n\\]\n\n---\n\n### 6.  Conclusion  \n\nCombining the impossibility result (3.1) with the explicit constructions of\nSections\u202f4 and\u202f5, we have proved that for every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) the set of\nadmissible numbers of sunny lines is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n-1}{2}\\bigr\\rfloor\\}\\;}.\n\\]\n\n\u220e"
