n \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\); therefore the set of covered points does not change, while the number of sunny lines drops from \\(n\\) to \\(n-1\\).\n\n#### 3.6  Conclusion of the induction  \n\nWe have shown that, assuming (7), the set \\(\\mathcal K_n\\) contains every integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\).  \nTogether with the base case \\(n=3\\) this proves (6) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final verification  \n\n*Existence.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (Sections 2.1, 2.2 and the inductive construction of Section 3).\n\n*Necessity of the bounds.*  \nBecause we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the only possible candidate, and we have proved that every element of this set is indeed attainable.\n\nThus the answer is complete and rigorous.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3.}\n\\]"
