            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\n---\n\n### 1.  Preliminaries  \n\nA line in the plane is called **sunny** iff its slope belongs to  \n\n\\[\n\\mathbb R\\setminus\\{0,\\infty,-1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) set  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe cardinality of \\(S_n\\) is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\sum_{t=2}^{n+1}(t-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nWe have to decide, for each \\(k\\in\\mathbb Z_{\\ge0}\\), whether there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) such that  \n\n* \\(\\displaystyle S_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines are sunny.\n\nDenote by \\(\\mathcal K_n\\) the set of all such \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n### 2.  Extremal values  \n\n#### 2.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nTake the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines in (3) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore none of them is sunny.  \nConsequently \\(0\\in\\mathcal K_n\\) for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(t\\) with \\(2\\le t\\le n+1\\) consider the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_t:\\;y=x+(t-2).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_t\\) is \\(1\\), which is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); thus every \\(\\ell_t\\) is sunny.\nThe line \\(\\ell_t\\) contains exactly the points of the diagonal  \n\n\\[\nD_t:=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb R^2\\mid a+b=t\\}.\n\\]\n\nIndeed, if \\((a,b)\\in D_t\\) then \\(b=t-a\\) and substituting in (4) gives \\(t-a=a+(t-2)\\), i.e. \\(b=a+(t-2)\\), which holds precisely when \\(b=t-a\\).  \nHence  \n\n\\[\nD_t\\cap\\mathbb Z^2=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a+b=t\\}\\subseteq\\ell_t .\n\\]\n\nSince the sets \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a+b=t\\}\\) for \\(t=2,\\dots ,n+1\\) form a partition of \\(S_n\\), the family  \n\n\\[\n\\{\\ell_t\\mid t=2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\ncovers all points of \\(S_n\\).  \nThere are exactly \\(n\\) lines in (5) and each of them is sunny, so \\(n\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Inductive construction for arbitrary \\(k\\)\n\nWe prove that  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K_n=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}}\\qquad\\text{for every }n\\ge3 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe proof proceeds by induction on \\(n\\).\n\n#### 3.1  Base step \\(n=3\\)\n\nThe set \\(S_3\\) consists of the six points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(1,3),\\;(2,2),\\;(3,1).\n\\]\n\nExplicit families of three lines realising each \\(k\\in\\{0,1,2,3\\}\\) are given below.\n\n| \\(k\\) | Lines (written as equations) | Sunny lines |\n|------|------------------------------|--------------|\n| 0 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\) | none |\n| 1 | \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;y=x+1\\) | \\(y=x+1\\) |\n| 2 | \\(x=1,\\;y=x,\\;y=x+2\\) | \\(y=x,\\;y=x+2\\) |\n| 3 | \\(y=x,\\;y=x+1,\\;y=x+2\\) | all three |\n\nEach family covers the six points, and the number of sunny lines is as indicated. Hence (6) holds for \\(n=3\\).\n\n#### 3.2  Inductive hypothesis  \n\nAssume that for some \\(n-1\\ge3\\) we already know  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal K_{\\,n-1}=\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus for every \\(k'\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\) there exists a family \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{\\,k'}\\) of \\(n-1\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n-1}\\) and containing exactly \\(k'\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.3  Construction for \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\nLet \\(k\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\).  \nTake the family \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{\\,k}\\) (which exists by the hypothesis) and add the vertical line  \n\n\\[\nL_{\\text{new}}:\\;x=n .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Coverage.*  \nAll points of \\(S_{n-1}\\) are already covered by \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{\\,k}\\).  \nThe new points that belong to \\(S_n\\) but not to \\(S_{n-1}\\) are exactly the points with \\(a=n\\) and \\(b=1\\); they lie on \\(L_{\\text{new}}\\).  \nHence the enlarged family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal F_n^{\\,k}:=\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{\\,k}\\cup\\{L_{\\text{new}}\\}\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\ncovers the whole set \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Sunny count.*  \n\\(L_{\\text{new}}\\) is vertical, therefore non‑sunny.  \nConsequently \\(\\mathcal F_n^{\\,k}\\) contains exactly the same number \\(k\\) of sunny lines as \\(\\mathcal F_{n-1}^{\\,k}\\).\n\nThus for every \\(k\\le n-1\\) we have produced a configuration with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n#### 3.4  Construction for \\(k=n\\)\n\nThe all‑sunny family described in Section 2.2 (the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\ell_t\\), \\(t=2,\\dots ,n+1\\)) belongs to \\(\\mathcal K_n\\). Hence \\(n\\in\\mathcal K_n\\).\n\n#### 3.5  Construction for \\(k=n-1\\) (optional, follows from the previous steps)\n\nStarting from the all‑sunny configuration of Section 2.2, replace the line \\(\\ell_{2}\\) (which is \\(y=x\\)) by the vertical line \\(x=1\\).  \nBoth lines contain the whole first column \\(\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\); therefore the set of covered points does not change, while the number of sunny lines drops from \\(n\\) to \\(n-1\\).\n\n#### 3.6  Conclusion of the induction  \n\nWe have shown that, assuming (7), the set \\(\\mathcal K_n\\) contains every integer from \\(0\\) up to \\(n\\).  \nTogether with the base case \\(n=3\\) this proves (6) for all \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Final verification  \n\n*Existence.*  \nFor each \\(k\\in\\{0,\\dots ,n\\}\\) we have exhibited an explicit family of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_n\\) and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines (Sections 2.1, 2.2 and the inductive construction of Section 3).\n\n*Necessity of the bounds.*  \nBecause we are required to use exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\). Hence the set \\(\\{0,1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) is the only possible candidate, and we have proved that every element of this set is indeed attainable.\n\nThus the answer is complete and rigorous.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\mathcal K_n=\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\qquad\\text{for all }n\\ge3.}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
