nts of \\(S_n\\) form a right\u2011angled triangular lattice; its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nWe shall prove that for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct lines\n\\(L_1,\\dots ,L_n\\) such that  \n\n* \\(\\displaystyle S_n\\subseteq\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}L_i\\);  \n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines satisfy (1).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Two extremal families  \n\n#### Lemma 1 (all lines non\u2011sunny).  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\ncover \\(S_n\\) and none of them is sunny.  \n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines in (4) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, therefore their slopes are \\(\\infty\\) and they are not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n#### Lemma 2 (all lines sunny).  \nFor every \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines covering \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Construction.*  \nDefine, for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\text{if }i\\text{ is odd:}&\\qquad\nL_i:\\; y = -\\bigl(\\tfrac{i+1}{2}\\bigr)\\,x + \\bigl(\\tfrac{i+1}{2}\\bigr)(n+2-\\tfrac{i+1}{2}),\\\\[2mm]\n\\text{if }i\\text{ is even:}&\\qquad\nL_i:\\; y = -\\frac{2}{i}\\,x + \\frac{2}{i}\\,(n+1).\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes occurring in (5) are negative rational numbers different from \\(-1\\); none equals \\(0\\) or \\(\\infty\\). Hence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are distinct.\n\n*Coverage.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le b\\) set \\(i=2a-1\\) (which is odd and satisfies \\(1\\le i\\le n\\) because \\(a\\le b\\le n+1-a\\)).  \nA direct substitution shows that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_i\\).  \n\nIf \\(a>b\\) set \\(i=2b\\) (which is even and lies between \\(2\\) and \\(n\\)).  \nAgain a direct su