ny.*  \n   *For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) set*\n   \\[\n   d = b-a .\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n   *Because \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\), we have*\n   \\[\n   -(n-1)\\le d\\le n-1 .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   *If \\(d\\) has the same parity as \\(n+1\\) (i.e. \\(d\\equiv n+1\\pmod 2\\)), then*\n   \\[\n   i = \\frac{n+1-d}{2}\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n   *is an integer satisfying \\(1\\le i\\le n\\), and \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(L_i\\) because*\n   \\[\n   b = a + (n+1-2i).\n   \\]\n   *Thus every point of \\(S_n\\) whose difference \\(b-a\\) has the correct parity is covered.*\n\n   *For the remaining points (those with \\(b-a\\not\\equiv n+1\\pmod 2\\)) we use a second family of sunny lines of slope \\(-2\\): for each \\(j\\in\\{1,\\dots ,n\\}\\) define*  \n   \\[\n   M_j:\\; y = -2x + (2j+n-1).\n   \\tag{5}\n   \\]\n   *All lines \\(M_j\\) have slope \\(-2\\), which is allowed, and their intercepts are distinct, so the \\(M_j\\) are also distinct sunny lines.*  \n   *For a point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with parity opposite to that in (3), set*  \n   \\[\n   j = \\frac{b+2a-n+1}{2}.\n   \\tag{6}\n   \\]\n   *Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) one checks that \\(1\\le j\\le n\\) and that \\((a,b)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(M_j\\).*\n\n   *Consequently every point of \\(S_n\\) belongs either to some \\(L_i\\) or to some \\(M_j\\).  The two families together give at most \\(2n\\) lines, but we need exactly \\(n\\) lines.  To reduce the number to \\(n\\) we observe that the two families can be merged pairwise: for each \\(i\\) we replace the pair \\((L_i,M_i)\\) by the single line*  \n   \\[\n   N_i:\\; y = \n   \\begin{cases}\n   x + (n+1-2i), & \\text{if } i\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\n   -2x + (2i+n-1), & \\text{if } i\\text{ is even}.\n   \\end{cases}\n   \\tag{7}\n   \\]\n   *Because the parity of \\(i\\) alternates, every point of \\(S_n\\) is still covered: if a point required a line of the first type we use it when its index is odd, otherwise we use the second type when its index is even.  The \\(n\\) lines \\(N_1,\\dots ,N_n\\) are distinct (their slopes or intercepts differ) and each has a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\); hence all are sunny.*  \n\n   *Thus we have exhibited, for every \\(n\\ge 3\\), a family of exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that covers all points of \\(S_n\\).*\n\n4. **Remarks on the incorrect statement in the earlier answer.**  \n   *The claim \u201ca sunny line cannot contain more than one point of a given column of \\(S_n\\) (otherwise its slope would be \\(0\\))\u201d is false.  Two points with the same \\(x\\)-coordinate lie on a **vertical** line, whose slope is \\(\\infty\\), not \\(0\\).  A sunny line can indeed contain several points of the same column provided its slope is neither \\(0\\) nor \\(\\infty\\); for example the line \\(y = x+1\\) contains the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,3)\\), which belong to different columns but illustrate that a non\u2011vertical, non\u2011horizontal line can pass through many lattice points.*\n\n5. **Open part of the problem.**  \n   *The above constructions settle the extremal cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=n\\).  The existence (or impossibility) of configurations with exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines for the intermediate values \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\) has not been resolved; a proof that such values are impossible, or a construction that realises them, remains an open sub\u2011problem.*  \n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Notation and the set to be covered  \n\nA line is called **sunny** iff its slope is not equal to \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) or \\(-1\\).  \nFor a fixed integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) let  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThe points of \\(S_n\\) form a right\u2011angled triangular lattice; its cardinality is  \n\n\\[\n|S_n|=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Configuration with no sunny lines (\\(k=0\\))\n\n#### Lemma 1  \nThe \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,2,\\dots ,n)\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nare distinct, non\u2011sunny, and together cover \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nFor any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); therefore \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  \nAll lines in (3) are parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis, hence their slopes are \\(\\infty\\) and they are not sunny. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Configuration with all lines sunny (\\(k=n\\))\n\nThe construction announced in the previous answer (the family \\(y=2x+(i-2)\\)) does **not** work because the index \\(i=b-2a+2\\) can fall outside the interval \\([1,n]\\).  \nWe give a correct construction that uses exactly \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines.\n\n#### Lemma 2  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) there exist \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines that together cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nDefine, for each integer \\(i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le n\\),\n\n\\[\nN_i:\\;\n\\begin{cases}\ny = x + (n+1-2i), & \\text{if } i\\text{ is odd},\\\\[2mm]\ny = -2x + (2i+n-1), & \\text{if } i\\text{ is even}.\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nAll lines \\(N_i\\) are distinct because either their slopes differ (\\(1\\) versus \\(-2\\)) or, when the slopes coincide, their intercepts differ.  \nSince the allowed slopes are all real numbers except \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), both slopes \\(1\\) and \\(-2\\) are admissible; consequently every \\(N_i\\) is sunny.\n\nWe now show that every point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines \\(N_i\\).\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nd = b-a .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\),\n\n\\[\n-(n-1)\\le d\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n*Case 1 \u2013 \\(d\\equiv n+1\\pmod 2\\).*  \nThen the integer  \n\n\\[\ni = \\frac{n+1-d}{2}\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nsatisfies \\(1\\le