ry point of \\(S_n\\),\n   and has precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\n   Since the construction works for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\),\n   all these values are attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad \nR_r=\\{(a,r)\\mid 1\\le a\\le n+1-r\\}\\;(1\\le r\\le n).\n\\]\n\nThe rows \\(R_r\\) are disjoint and  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{r=1}^{n}R_r ,\\qquad |R_r|=n+1-r .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  Keeping the longest rows\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\nKeep the \\(n-k\\) longest rows, i.e. the rows  \n\n\\[\nR_1,R_2,\\dots ,R_{\\,n-k}.\n\\]\n\nCover them by the horizontal lines  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal H=\\{y=r\\mid 1\\le r\\le n-k\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny (they are parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis).\n\nThe remaining rows are  \n\n\\[\nR_{\\,n-k+1},R_{\\,n-k+2},\\dots ,R_{\\,n},\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nwhose lengths are \\(1,2,\\dots ,k\\) respectively.  \nHence the number of points still uncovered is  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{j=1}^{k}j=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Sunny lines that cover the short rows\n\nFor each \\(i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y=2x+(n-k-i).\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of every \\(L_i\\) equals \\(2\\); consequently each \\(L_i\\) is\n*sunny* (it is not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to\n\\(x+y=0\\)).  The lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) are distinct because their\nintercepts \\(n-k-i\\) are distinct.\n\nConsider a row \\(R_{\\,n-k+j}\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k\\).\nSolving (10) together with the equation \\(y=n-k+j\\) gives\n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{i+j}{2}.\n\\tag{11}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_i\\) meets the row \\(R_{\\,n-k+j}\\) in an integer point iff\n\\(i+j\\) is even; in that case the intersection