"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have completely solved the problem.  \nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\,k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\,}.\n\\]\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Notation.**  \n   Let  \n\n   \\[\n   S_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The required \\(n\\) lines must cover all points of \\(S_n\\).\n\n2. **Lemma (partition of \\(S_t\\) by sunny lines).**  \n   For any integer \\(t\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n   \\[\n   D_t=\\{-(t-1),-(t-3),\\dots ,t-3,t-1\\},\\qquad |D_t|=t .\n   \\]\n\n   For each \\(d\\in D_t\\) set  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_d:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_t\\mid a-b=d\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   Then  \n\n   * each \\(\\ell_d\\) is a line of slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny);\n   * the sets \\(\\ell_d\\;(d\\in D_t)\\) are pairwise disjoint;\n   * \\(\\displaystyle S_t=\\bigcup_{d\\in D_t}\\ell_d\\).\n\n3. **Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\).**  \n   Write \\(k\\) for the desired number of sunny lines and put \\(m=n-k\\).\n\n   * Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n     \\[\n     x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n     \\]\n\n     They are not sunny and together they cover all points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate is \\(\\le m\\).\n\n   * The remaining points are  \n\n     \\[\n     S'_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n     \\]\n\n     With the change of variables \\(a'=a-m\\) (so \\(a'\\ge 1\\)) we obtain  \n\n     \\[\n     S'_k=\\{(a',b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a'+b\\le k+1\\}=S_k .\n     \\]\n\n     By the Lemma, \\(S_k\\) is covered by the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_d\\;(d\\in D_k)\\).\n\n   * Altogether we have \\(m\\) non\u2011sunny vertical lines and \\(k\\) sunny slope\u2011\\(1\\) lines, i.e. exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines, of which precisely \\(k\\) are sunny, and every point of \\(S_n\\) lies on at least one of them.\n\n4. **Extremal cases.**  \n   *\\(k=0\\):* the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  \n   *\\(k=n\\):* the \\(n\\) sunny lines of slope \\(1\\) given by the Lemma.\n\n5. **Impossibility of other values.**  \n   The number of sunny lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines must be an integer between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\). The construction above shows that every integer in this interval can actually be realised. Hence no other values of \\(k\\) are possible.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1 (partition of \\(S_t\\) by sunny lines)\n\nFor any integer \\(t\\ge 1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nD_t=\\{-(t-1),-(t-3),\\dots ,t-3,t-1\\},\n\\qquad |D_t|=t .\n\\]\n\nFor each \\(d\\in D_t\\) set  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_t\\mid a-b=d\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen  \n\n1. each \\(\\ell_d\\) is a line of slope \\(1\\) (hence sunny);\n2. the sets \\(\\ell_d\\;(d\\in D_t)\\) are pairwise disjoint;\n3. \\(\\displaystyle S_t=\\bigcup_{d\\in D_t}\\ell_d\\).\n\n*Proof.*  \nIf \\((a,b)\\in S_t\\) then \\(a+b\\le t+1\\).  \nWriting \\(d=a-b\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n-(t-1)\\le d\\le t-1,\\qquad d\\equiv t-1\\pmod 2 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\(d\\in D_t\\) and \\((a,b)\\in\\ell_d\\). Hence every point of \\(S_t\\) belongs to some \\(\\ell_d\\), proving the union equals \\(S_t\\).  \n\nConversely, if \\(a-b=d\\) with \\(d\\in D_t\\) and \\(a,b>0\\), then  \n\n\\[\na+b=(a-b)+2b=d+2b\\le (t-1)+2b\\le t+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\in S_t\\). Different values of \\(d\\) give different lines, so the \\(\\ell_d\\) are distinct. \u220e\n\n\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 0).\n\\]\n\n**Step\u202f1 \u2013 non\u2011sunny vertical lines.**  \nTake