ll_d\\) is sunny.\n\n2. If \\(d_1\\neq d_2\\) then the equations \\(x-y=d_1\\) and \\(x-y=d_2\\) cannot be satisfied simultaneously; hence \\(\\ell_{d_1}\\cap\\ell_{d_2}=\\varnothing\\).\n\n3. *Inclusion \\( \\subseteq\\).*  \n   Take any \\((x,y)\\in S_t\\).  Because \\(x,y>0\\) and \\(x+y\\le t+1\\), we have  \n\n   \\[\n   -(t-1)\\le x-y\\le t-1,\n   \\qquad\n   x-y\\equiv t-1\\pmod 2 .\n   \\]\n\n   The first inequality follows from \\(x+y\\le t+1\\) together with \\(x,y\\ge1\\); the parity condition follows because \\(x+y\\) and \\(x-y\\) have the same parity, and \\(x+y\\) has the same parity as \\(t+1\\).  Hence \\(d:=x-y\\) belongs to \\(D_t\\), and \\((x,y)\\in\\ell_d\\).  Thus every point of \\(S_t\\) lies in some \\(\\ell_d\\).\n\n   *Inclusion \\( \\supseteq\\).*  \n   Conversely, let \\((x,y)\\in\\ell_d\\) for some \\(d\\in D_t\\).  By definition \\(x-y=d\\) and \\(x,y>0\\).  Since \\(d\\le t-1\\),\n\n   \\[\n   x+y=(x-y)+2y=d+2y\\le (t-1)+2y\\le t+1,\n   \\]\n\n   because \\(y\\ge1\\).  Hence \\((x,y)\\in S_t\\).\n\n   Combining the two inclusions yields \\(S_t=\\bigcup_{d\\in D_t}\\ell_d\\). ∎\n\n\n\n---\n\n### 3.  Construction for a given number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).  Define  \n\n\\[\nm:=n-k\\qquad(\\text{the number of non‑sunny lines}).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nWe treat the two extreme values separately and then give a uniform construction for the remaining cases.\n\n#### 3.1  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nHere \\(m=n\\).  Choose the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,n).\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach line is not sunny (its slope is \\(\\infty\\)).  For any \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) we have \\(1\\le a\\le n\\); consequently \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).  Hence all points of \\(S_n\\) are covered and the required configuration exists.\n\n#### 3.2  The case \\(k=n\\)\n\nNow \\(m=0\\); we need only sunny lines.  By Lemma 1 with \\(t=n\\),\n\n\\[\nS_n=\\bigcup_{d\\in D_n}\\ell_d,\n\\]\n\nwhere each \\(\\ell_d\\) is a sunny line of slope \\(1\\).  Since \\(|D_n|=n\\), the \\(n\\) lines \\(\\{\\ell_d\\mid d\\in D_n\\}\\) satisfy the conditions.\n\n#### 3.3  General case \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\n*Non‑sunny part.*  \nTake the \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;x=i\\qquad(i=1,\\dots ,m).\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese lines are not sunny.  Any point \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\) lies on the line \\(x=a\\).\n\n*Sunny part.*  \nConsider the subset of points of \\(S_n\\) whose first coordinate exceeds \\(m\\):\n\n\\[\nS'_k:=\\{(a,b)\\in S_n\\mid a\\ge m+1\\}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine \\(a':=a-m\\) (so \\(a'\\ge1\\)).  Because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) and \\(n+1=m+k+1\\),\n\n\\[\na'+b=(a-m)+b\\le (n+1)-m = k+1.\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a',b)\\in S_k\\).  Conversely, if \\((a',b)\\in S_k\\) then \\(a:=a'+m\\) satisfies \\(a\\ge m+1\\) and  \n\n\\[\na+b=(a'+m)+b\\le (k+1)+m=n+1,\n\\]\n\nso \\((a,b)\\in S'_k\\).  Hence we have proved the equality of the two sets:\n\n\\[\nS'_k = S_k.\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Covering \\(S_k\\) by sunny lines.*  \nApply Lemma 1 with \\(t=k\\).  It yields a family of \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_d:=\\{(x,y)\\in S_k\\mid x-y=d\\},\\qquad d\\in D_k,\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nwhich are pairwise disjoint and satisfy  \n\n\\[\nS_k=\\bigcup_{d\\in D_k}\\ell_d .\n\\tag{10}\n\\]\n\nBecause each \\(\\ell_d\\) has slope \\(1\\), it is sunny (slope \\(1\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\)).\n\n*Putting everything together.*  \nThe total collection of lines consists of  \n\n* the \\(m=n-k\\) vertical lines \\(L_i\\) (non‑sunny), and  \n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_d\\) (\\(d\\in D_k\\)).\n\nAll these lines are distinct because the vertical lines have undefined slope while the \\(\\ell_d\\) have slope \\(1\\).  The number of lines is \\(m+k=n\\), and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.\n\n*Verification that every point of \\(S_n\\) is covered.*  \n\n* If \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\le m\\), then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (one of the \\(L_i\\)).\n* If \\((a,b)\\in S_n\\) with \\(a\\ge m+1\\), then \\((a,b)\\in S'_k=S_k\\) by (8); consequently \\((a,b)\\) belongs to the unique line \\(\\ell_d\\) for which \\(a-m-b=d\\) (by (10)).  Hence it is covered by a sunny line.\n\nThus every required point is on at least one of the chosen lines.\n\n---\n\n### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nFor any integer \\(n\\ge3\\) and any integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited an explicit set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying:\n\n* every point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) lies on at least one of the lines;\n* exactly \\(k\\) of the lines have slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\) (i.e. are sunny).\n\nNo other value of \\(k\\) is possible because the number of sunny lines cannot exceed the total number of lines \\(n\\) nor be negative.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
