            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the *diagonal*\n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nThen \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s ,\\qquad\n\\bigl| \\mathcal P_n \\bigr|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A property of sunny lines  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).  \nIf a sunny line \\(L\\) meets a diagonal \\(D_s\\) in two distinct points,\nthose two points would lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the\ndefinition of a sunny line. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line meets each diagonal }D_s\\text{ in at most one point.}}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Lower bound on the number of non‑sunny lines  \n\nIf a diagonal \\(D_s\\) is **not** covered by a line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\),\nthen, by (4), each point of \\(D_s\\) must be supplied by a different line.\nConsequently, to avoid using a non‑sunny line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) we would need at least \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) distinct lines for that diagonal.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Construction for an arbitrary \\(k\\)  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nd=n-k .\n\\]\n\n#### 3.1  The \\(d\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\nFor the integers  \n\n\\[\ns=n+2-d,\\;n+3-d,\\dots ,n+1\n\\]\n\nconsider the lines  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is non‑sunny.\nThe set of diagonals covered by the family \\(\\{\\ell_s\\}\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+2-d},D_{n+3-d},\\dots ,D_{n+1},\n\\]\n\ni.e. the \\(d\\) *largest* diagonals.  Every point of those diagonals lies on\none of the lines (5).\n\n#### 3.2  The remaining \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nThe diagonals that are still uncovered are  \n\n\\[\nD_2,D_3,\\dots ,D_{k+1}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nTheir total number of points equals  \n\n\\[\n\\sum_{s=2}^{k+1}|D_s|=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nDefine, for each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = i\\,x + (1-i) .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_i\\) is \\(i\\neq0,\\infty,-1\\); hence each \\(L_i\\) is sunny,\nand the slopes are pairwise distinct, so the lines are distinct.\n\n*Intersection with a diagonal.*  \nLet a point of \\(D_s\\) be \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a+b=s\\). Substituting \\(b=s-a\\) into (8) gives\n\n\\[\ns-a = i a + (1-i)\n\\;\\Longleftrightarrow\\;\na = \\frac{s+i-1}{2},\\qquad\nb = \\frac{s-i+1}{2}.\n\\tag{9}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\) and \\(1\\le i\\le k\\), the numerator \\(s+i-1\\) is an\ninteger of the same parity as \\(2\\); therefore the right–hand sides of (9)\nare integers. Moreover \\(a>0\\) and \\(b>0\\) (they are at most \\(k\\)), so the\npair \\((a,b)\\) belongs to \\(D_s\\).\n\nEquation (9) shows that for each ordered pair \\((i,s)\\) with\n\\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(2\\le s\\le k+1\\) the line \\(L_i\\) meets the diagonal\n\\(D_s\\) in the unique lattice point \\((\\frac{s+i-1}{2},\\frac{s-i+1}{2})\\).\nConversely, every point of the diagonals (6) appears for exactly one such\npair \\((i,s)\\). Hence the union of the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) is\nprecisely the set (6).\n\n#### 3.3  Verification of the whole configuration  \n\nWe have produced  \n\n* \\(d=n-k\\) non‑sunny lines (5) covering the diagonals\n  \\(D_{n+2-d},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\\);\n* \\(k\\) sunny lines (8) covering the remaining diagonals (6).\n\nAll \\(n\\) lines are distinct, exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny, and every\npoint of \\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the lines.  Thus a\nconfiguration satisfying the statement exists for the chosen \\(k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Exhaustion of the possibilities  \n\nSince the construction works for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\),\nall those values are admissible.  \nConversely, a configuration contains exactly \\(n\\) lines, so the number of\nsunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Hence the set of\nall possible \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Thus, for any integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the admissible values of \\(k\\) are exactly the\nnon‑negative integers not exceeding \\(n\\).**\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
