            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) define the *diagonal*\n\n\\[\nD_s=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\mid a+b=s\\}.\n\\]\n\nClearly \\(|D_s|=s-1\\) and  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_n=\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s ,\\qquad\n\\bigl| \\mathcal P_n \\bigr|=\\sum_{s=2}^{n+1}(s-1)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A property of sunny lines  \n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\nIf a sunny line met a diagonal \\(D_s\\) in two distinct points,\nthose two points would determine a line of slope \\(-1\\), contradicting the\ndefinition of a sunny line. Hence\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{A sunny line meets each diagonal }D_s\\text{ in at most one point.}}\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for a given \\(k\\)  \n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nd:=n-k .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.1  The \\(d\\) non‑sunny diagonal lines  \n\nConsider the integers  \n\n\\[\ns=n+2-d,\\;n+3-d,\\dots ,n+1 .\n\\]\n\nFor each such \\(s\\) take the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_s:\\;x+y=s .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nEach \\(\\ell_s\\) is parallel to the line \\(x+y=0\\); therefore it is\nnon‑sunny.  The family \\(\\{\\ell_s\\}\\) contains exactly \\(d\\) distinct\nlines and covers the diagonals  \n\n\\[\nD_{n+2-d},\\;D_{n+3-d},\\dots ,D_{n+1}\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\ncompletely, because every point of those diagonals satisfies the equation\n\\(x+y=s\\) for the appropriate \\(s\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.2  The \\(k\\) sunny lines  \n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_i:\\;y = x-(i-1) .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of each \\(L_i\\) equals \\(1\\), which is different from\n\\(0,\\infty,-1\\); consequently every \\(L_i\\) is sunny and the \\(k\\) lines\nare distinct.\n\n*Claim.*  Every point \\((a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_n\\) with \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) lies\non exactly one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\) satisfy \\(a,b>0\\) and \\(a+b\\le k+1\\).  Set  \n\n\\[\ni:=a-b+1 .\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(a+b\\le k+1\\) we have \\(-k+1\\le a-b\\le k-1\\); hence\n\\(1\\le i\\le k\\).  Moreover \\(a-b=i-1\\), i.e. \\(b=a-(i-1)\\).  Substituting\nthis relation into (7) gives  \n\n\\[\ny = x-(i-1)\\;\\Longrightarrow\\;b = a-(i-1),\n\\]\n\nwhich is precisely the equality satisfied by \\((a,b)\\).  Thus\n\\((a,b)\\in L_i\\).  Uniqueness follows from (4): a sunny line meets a\ngiven diagonal in at most one point, and the index \\(i\\) is determined\nby the difference \\(a-b\\).  ∎\n\nConsequently the union of the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) equals  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{s=2}^{k+1} D_s .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.3  Verification that every required point is covered  \n\nFrom (6) and (8) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\bigcup_{s=2}^{n+1} D_s\n=\n\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{s=2}^{k+1} D_s\\Bigr)\n\\;\\cup\\;\n\\Bigl(\\bigcup_{s=n+2-d}^{n+1} D_s\\Bigr) .\n\\]\n\nThe first union is covered by the sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\);\nthe second union is covered by the non‑sunny diagonal lines\n\\(\\ell_{\\,n+2-d},\\dots ,\\ell_{\\,n+1}\\).  Hence **every** point of\n\\(\\mathcal P_n\\) lies on at least one of the constructed lines.\n\nThe total number of distinct lines is  \n\n\\[\nd\\;+\\;k\\;=\\;(n-k)+k\\;=\\;n,\n\\]\n\nand exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_i\\) are sunny.  Therefore for the chosen\n\\(k\\) we have produced a configuration satisfying both conditions of the\nproblem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Necessity of the range for \\(k\\)\n\nAny admissible configuration consists of exactly \\(n\\) distinct lines,\nso the number of sunny lines cannot be negative and cannot exceed \\(n\\).\nThus \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is necessary.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) and every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\)\nthere exists a set of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two requirements\nof the problem and containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.  No other value\nof \\(k\\) is possible.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
